On 2010 August 19, gridding observations of the pulsar position were performed at 2.1\\,\\mathrm{GHz} to improve the uncertainty of the discovery sky position. We used a square grid centered on the discovery position with nine observation pointings. The pulsar was found with equal signal-to-noise ratio in two of the gridding pointings. The improved sky position half-way between these two pointings is \\alpha=19^{\\rm h}52^{\\rm m}34.\\!^{\\rm s}4, \\delta=+26^{\\circ}30^{\\prime}14^{\\prime\\prime} with an uncertainty of \\approx 1^{\\prime} given by the telescope beam size.

Figure 1 shows the folded pulse profile of PSR J1952+2630 obtained from the 576 s follow-up observation on 2010 September 19. The full width at half maximum duty cycle is 6%, corresponding to a width of the pulse of w_{50}=1.3\\,\\mathrm{ms}. The dispersive delay across a frequency channel is 0.3\\,\\mathrm{ms} for the given DM, frequency resolution and central frequency. We calibrate the profile using the radiometer equation to predict the observation system's noise level. Since the sky position is known to 1^{\\prime} accuracy, we can only derive limits on the flux density. We obtain a system equivalent flux density S_{\\rm sys} of 3.0-3.8 Jy (depending on the precise pulsar position within the beam). The observation bandwidth is 172\\,\\mathrm{MHz}, the observation time is 576\\,\\mathrm{s}, and the folded profile has N=128 bins; then the expected off-pulse noise standard deviation used for calibration is 76-98 \\muJy. The estimated period-averaged flux density of the pulsar at 1.4\\,\\mathrm{GHz} is 70\\,\\mu\\mathrm{Jy}\\leq S_{1400}\\leq 100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{Jy} (depending on its position within the beam).

## 4. Spin period-based orbital solution

We obtain a solution for the orbital parameters based on measurements of the barycentric spin period and spin period derivative. The pulsar's orbital motion in a binary system causes changes of the apparent barycentric spin frequency over time due to the Doppler effect. For a circular orbit, the barycentric spin period P as a function of the orbital phase \\theta=\\Omega_{\\rm orb}\\left(t-T_{\\rm asc}\\right) measured from the time of passage of the ascending node T_{\\rm asc} is given by

P\\left(\\theta\\right)=P_{0}\\left(1+x\\Omega_{\\rm orb}\\cos\\left(\\theta\\right) \\right). 

